WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.276
Socket programs need a clear trust model.

2
00:00:02.276 --> 00:00:09.430
A client and server may run on the same host, across a private network,
across the public internet, or through proxies and gateways.

3
00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:15.414
A peer may be an internal service, an external partner, an unmanaged
device, or a process controlled by a user.

4
00:00:15.414 --> 00:00:20.487
Network location can influence risk, but it should not be treated as proof
of trust by itself.

5
00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:23.804
The data arriving on a socket is bytes from another endpoint.

6
00:00:23.804 --> 00:00:27.250
Those bytes are not automatically a trusted protocol message.

7
00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:38.502
The application has to decide how to interpret them, which state the
connection is in, whether the peer is authenticated, whether the requested
operation is authorized, and how to handle unexpected input.

8
00:00:38.502 --> 00:00:45.591
A useful protocol treats incoming bytes as untrusted until they are
framed, parsed, validated, and matched to an allowed action.

9
00:00:45.591 --> 00:00:47.607
Protocol design should be explicit.

10
00:00:47.607 --> 00:00:57.948
A socket service should define message boundaries, versioning
expectations, authentication steps, command names or message types,
allowed states, error behavior, and close behavior.

11
00:00:57.948 --> 00:01:05.817
Informal assumptions such as the client always sends this first or the
internal peer never sends malformed data tend to break as systems evolve.

12
00:01:05.817 --> 00:01:09.850
A protocol state machine, even a simple one, makes expectations visible.

13
00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:14.337
Authentication and authorization are separate from connection
establishment.

14
00:01:14.337 --> 00:01:18.565
A connected peer is not automatically allowed to perform sensitive
actions.

15
00:01:18.565 --> 00:01:28.840
After the connection is established, the application still needs to
identify the peer at an appropriate level and enforce permissions per
command, message, tenant, device, or resource.

16
00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.767
Internal services should follow the same principle.

17
00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:36.840
A private network can reduce exposure, but it does not replace application
trust decisions.
